  "response": "**Calculation:**\n\n- Prevalence: P(D) = 1/500 = 0.002, P(¬D) = 0.998\n- Sensitivity: P(+|D) = 0.98\n- Specificity: P(+|¬D) = 1 − 0.95 = 0.05\n\nBy Bayes' theorem:\n\nP(D|+) = [0.98 × 0.002] / [0.98 × 0.002 + 0.05 × 0.998]\n    = 0.00196 / (0.00196 + 0.0499)\n    = 0.00196 / 0.05186\n    ≈ 0.037794 → **3.78%**\n\nSo even with a positive test result, there's only about a 3.78% chance the person actually has the condition — a classic illustration of how low prevalence keeps the false-positive count high relative to true positives.\n\nFINAL ANSWER: 3.78",
